```

By setting the locale to English, you are ensuring that the dayjs library interprets the date string in the correct format (day-month-year) and returns the correct date object (April 7, 2024) according to the specified format.

If you receive a string '07.04.2024' and want dayjs to understand that it is in the DD.MM.YYYY format, you can explicitly specify the format when parsing the date string. Here's how you can achieve this:

```javascript
import dayjs from 'dayjs';
import customParseFormat from 'dayjs/plugin/customParseFormat';

dayjs.extend(customParseFormat); // extend dayjs with customParseFormat plugin

const date = dayjs('07.04.2024', 'DD.MM.YYYY').toDate();
console.log(date);
```

By using the customParseFormat plugin, you can pass the custom format 'DD.MM.YYYY' as a parameter to the dayjs function, ensuring that dayjs correctly interprets the date string in the specified format and returns the correct date object (April 7, 2024).

